**`circuit_breaker.rs`:**
```rust
use tokio::time::{sleep, Duration};
use tracing::{info, warn};

pub struct CircuitBreaker {
    state: State,
    failure_threshold: usize,
    recovery_timeout: Duration,
}

enum State {
    Closed,
    Open(usize),
}

impl CircuitBreaker {
    pub fn new(failure_threshold: usize, recovery_timeout: Duration) -> Self {
        CircuitBreaker {
            state: State::Closed,
            failure_threshold,
            recovery_timeout,
        }
    }

    pub async fn call<F>(&mut self, mut f: F) -> Result<(), &'static str>
    where
        F: FnMut() -> Result<(), &'static str>,
    {
        match &self.state {
            State::Closed => {
                if let Err(_) = f() {
                    info!("Request failed, incrementing failure count");
                    self.state = State::Open(1);
                }
                Ok(())
            },
            State::Open(failures) if *failures < self.failure_threshold => {
                warn!("Circuit breaker is open; retrying after delay...");
                sleep(self.recovery_timeout).await;
                if let Err(_) = f() {
                    *failures += 1;
                } else {
                    self.state = State::Closed;
                }
                Ok(())
            },
            _ => Err("Service unavailable, circuit breaker open"),
        }
    }
}
```

This code is modular and extensible. You can add new tasks by creating functions in `tasks.rs` and updating the main function's match statement. The logging configuration uses `log4rs` to create a rotating log file system that ensures logs are rotated and stored efficiently without affecting performance.

The application includes graceful shutdown capabilities using Tokio's signal handling, allowing it to terminate cleanly on receiving termination signals. It also demonstrates how to use an in-memory option or persist data via disk storage by adding configuration options for task persistence within `tasks.rs`.']

