import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.Queue;
import java.util.LinkedList;

public class ConnectionManager {

    private final int maxConnections;
    private final long reconnectInterval;
    private final ExecutorService executor;
    private final BlockingQueue<Connection> connectionPool;
    private volatile boolean running = true;

    public ConnectionManager(int maxConnections, long reconnectInterval) {
        this.maxConnections = maxConnections;
        this.reconnectInterval = reconnectInterval;
        this.connectionPool = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(maxConnections);
        this.executor = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();
    }

    public void start() {
        for (int i = 0; i < maxConnections; i++) {
            createAndManageConnection();
        }
    }

    private void createAndManageConnection() {
        executor.submit(() -> {
            while (running) {
                try (Connection conn = establishConnection()) {
                    connectionPool.put(conn);

                    // Simulate work with the connection
                    handleConnection(conn);

                    // Handle reconnection logic
                } catch (InterruptedException | IOException e) {
                    notifyBroker(e);
                    Thread.sleep(reconnectInterval);
                }
            }
        });
    }

    private Connection establishConnection() throws IOException {
        // Implement actual connection establishment logic here
        return new Connection();
    }

    private void handleConnection(Connection conn) {
        // Process the connection, e.g., read/write messages
    }

    private void notifyBroker(Exception e) {
        // Integrate with an external message broker for error handling
    }

    public Connection getConnection() throws InterruptedException {
        return connectionPool.poll(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }

    public void shutdown() {
        running = false;
        executor.shutdown();
    }

    static class Connection implements AutoCloseable {
        @Override
        public void close() throws Exception {
            // Close the connection
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int maxConnections = 100; // Configurable pool size
        long reconnectInterval = 5000; // Reconnect interval in milliseconds

        ConnectionManager manager = new ConnectionManager(maxConnections, reconnectInterval);
        manager.start();

        Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(manager::shutdown));
    }
}
```

This Java code outlines a basic framework for managing connections to a high-traffic web service. It includes connection pooling with configurable size and reconnection logic that handles failed connections by attempting to reconnect after a specified interval. The `ExecutorService` is used to handle concurrent connection management tasks, ensuring thread safety.

The code also integrates a placeholder method `notifyBroker` to report errors or status updates to an external message broker, which can be expanded based on specific requirements of the system's communication with such brokers.

The solution ensures scalability and modularity by leveraging Java’s concurrency utilities and keeping the design modular for easier maintenance and enhancement. Adjustments in pool size and reconnect intervals should be made according to observed network conditions and load testing results."]

